.Dd August 4, 2026
.Dt T150BOOT 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm t150boot
.Nd take a Thrustmaster wheel out of boot mode
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nq
.Op Fl v Ar vid
.Op Fl p Ar pid
.Op Fl V Ar value
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Every T-series wheel enumerates at the shared boot product id
.Li 044f:b65d
and only reveals its own identity, and its own protocol, after a pair of
vendor control transfers on endpoint 0.
.Nm
issues them. Nothing else in this project works until it has.
.Pp
Sleep, wake and a replug all put the wheel back into boot mode, so this is
meant to run on every plug-in rather than once. It needs no privilege, so it
can be a user LaunchAgent that never asks for a password.
.Pp
It has to run before the game, not merely before the force feedback.
DirectInput builds a device's identity out of its vendor and product ids, so
a wheel enumerated at
.Li 044f:b65d
is a different device from the same wheel at
.Li 044f:b677 :
it appears as the generic
.Dq Thrustmaster FFB Wheel
that every T-series wheel shares rather than as the T150, and a game that
binds to it has every button mapped against a device it will not see next
time. Nothing inside the bottle can correct that afterwards, because CrossOver
carries in whatever macOS enumerated.
.Pp
.Xr t150d 8
does the same switch on its own scan, and
.Xr crossover-wheel 7
does it from the menu bar on a timer, so either of those running is enough.
.Pp
It never claims a USB interface. An endpoint 0 device request does not need
one, claiming the interface macOS's HID driver already owns would be refused,
and
.Fn libusb_claim_interface
is currently reported to panic macOS 26.3 on Apple silicon.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl n
Query the model and stop. Switches nothing.
.It Fl q
Say nothing unless something goes wrong, which is what a LaunchAgent wants.
.It Fl v Ar vid
Vendor id. The default is 0x044f.
.It Fl p Ar pid
The boot product id to look for. The default is 0xb65d.
.It Fl V Ar value
The switch value, which selects the model. The default is 0x0006, the T150
row of the kernel driver's table. Another wheel needs its own, and giving
this is also how you say you meant it: a wheel whose model byte is not the
T150's is refused without it, rather than being sent another model's value.
For such a wheel the check that it came back is skipped too, since nothing
here knows which product id to expect. On a T150 the check runs either way.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Exits 0 when the wheel was switched and came back at its firmware id, and
also when no wheel was found at the boot id, because that is the ordinary
state once one has been switched and a tool that runs on every plug-in must
not treat it as a failure. Exits 1 when a wheel was found and could not be
switched, when it was switched and never reappeared, and when it is not a
T150 and no
.Fl V
was given. Exits 2 on a usage error.
.Pp
It waits up to three seconds for the wheel to come back, because the switch
transfer's own result cannot say whether it worked: the wheel leaves the bus
before the transfer completes, so the errors that produces are
indistinguishable from a switch that went nowhere.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl $ t150boot
.Dl attachment 0x06, model 0x03  T150
.Dl switched, the wheel is at 0xb677
.Pp
Check what a wheel is without touching it:
.Pp
.Dl $ t150boot -n
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr t150ctl 1 ,
.Xr t150d 8 ,
.Xr t150-probe 1
.Sh CAVEATS
The wheel leaves the bus the instant it accepts the switch, so the transfer
usually cannot complete. A stall, a not-responding or a no-device from it is
the normal outcome and is not treated as a failure; what settles it is
whether the wheel reappears at its firmware id, which this waits for.
.Pp
This does not send the five initialisation packets the Linux driver puts on
the interrupt OUT pipe before the switch. Those need the device captured,
which needs root, and
.Nm probe_intr Fl I
is the tool for it. Whether they matter is unsettled: they were adopted here
to explain a wheel that came back apparently blocked, and that turned out to
be the autocenter holding at full strength rather than anything about the
switch. If a wheel misbehaves after this, try
.Nm t150ctl autocenter 0
before reaching for them.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Renaud Allard
